{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"taut-meritocrat-warbler","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# `rebar search` does not match tickets by their identifiers (ticket id, alias, or bound Jira key)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`rebar search` is full-text over a narrow haystack. `reducer/search.py::_haystack`\nbuilds the searchable text from ONLY `title`, `description`, `tags`, and each comment\n`body`. It excludes every IDENTIFIER a user is most likely to paste into search:\n\n- the canonical `ticket_id` (e.g. `0303-692c-55dc-4a18`),\n- the human `alias` (e.g. `doctorial-semiironic-wrasse`),\n- the bound **Jira key** (e.g. `REB-1654`).\n\nConcrete footgun (observed 2026-08-04): `rebar show REB-1654` correctly resolves to\n`0303-692c-55dc-4a18` (via `_ids.py::resolve_ticket_id` -> `_resolve_via_binding_store`),\nbut `rebar search \"REB-1654\"` returns `[]`. A user who reaches for `search` with an\nidentifier gets nothing and may wrongly conclude the ticket does not exist. The same holds\nfor a full ticket id or an alias substring.\n\n## Root-cause mechanism (cited)\n\n- `_haystack` never adds `ticket_id`/`alias`, and the reduced state carries NO Jira key at\n  all: the Jira binding lives in the binding store, read only by\n  `_ids.py::_resolve_via_binding_store` (the `.bridge_state` reverse map ticket_id -> jira_key).\n- `reducer/search.py::search_states` is deliberately pure (operates on state dicts); the\n  enrichment seam is the caller `_engine_support/reads.py::search_state` (line ~456), which\n  already injects a `parent_resolver`: the analogous place to fold identifiers in.\n- Matching is case-folded: `_haystack` returns `.lower()`. Any identifier added to the\n  haystack must be lowercased, AND a query typed as `REB-1654` (uppercase) must still match:\n  verify `_query.parse_query` lowercases free-text terms; if it does not, lower the compared\n  term for the id path so `show`/`search` accept the same input casing.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Extend `_haystack` (or add an identifier segment it concatenates) to include\n   `state.get(\"ticket_id\")`, `state.get(\"alias\")`, and a new optional\n   `state.get(\"jira_key\")` field, all lowercased, matching existing behavior.\n2. Enrich states with their bound Jira key in the caller `search_state`\n   (`_engine_support/reads.py`): build the ticket_id -> jira_key map ONCE from the binding\n   store's reverse map (the same source `_resolve_via_binding_store` reads) and annotate each\n   reduced state before calling `search_states`. Keep `search_states` pure: do not make it\n   read the filesystem/binding store.\n3. Guarantee case-insensitivity for identifier queries so `search REB-1654` and\n   `search reb-1654` both surface the bound ticket.\n\nDo NOT change the predicate/filter grammar (`_query.parse_query`, `_filters`): this is a\nhaystack widening only. Do NOT special-case identifier matches as \"exact\"; folding them into\nthe substring haystack is sufficient and keeps one matching path.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/reducer/search.py` - `_haystack` gains the identifier segment.\n- `src/rebar/_engine_support/reads.py` - `search_state` enriches states with `jira_key`.\n- A tiny accessor to read the binding reverse map without duplicating `_ids` internals\n  (reuse the existing one if present; cite it).\n- Tests below.\n\n## Testing (RED first - each fails on current main for the right reason)\n\n- `search_states` unit: a state carrying `ticket_id`/`alias`/`jira_key` is returned when the\n  query is that ticket_id, that alias, or that Jira key (each its own case), and is NOT\n  returned for an unrelated identifier. Drive the match, not a mock.\n- case-insensitivity: querying the UPPERCASE Jira key (`REB-1654`) matches a state whose\n  bound key is `REB-1654` (proves the fold on both sides).\n- integration at the `search_state`/CLI seam: a ticket bound to a Jira key is discoverable by\n  `rebar search <JIRA-KEY>` (mirrors `rebar show <JIRA-KEY>` resolving it): the exact\n  reported footgun, RED-verified by reverting the enrichment.\n- pinned-unchanged: existing free-text search over title/description/comments/tags returns\n  the same results (no regression to the historical whitespace-AND substring path); the\n  predicate grammar tests stay green.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar search` matches a ticket by its canonical `ticket_id`, its `alias`, and its\n      bound Jira key - checked by new unit tests on `search_states` (RED-first) and an\n      integration test at the `search_state` seam mirroring `show <JIRA-KEY>`.\n- [ ] identifier matching is case-insensitive (`REB-1654` and `reb-1654` both match) -\n      checked by a dedicated test.\n- [ ] `search_states` stays pure (no filesystem/binding-store access inside it); the Jira-key\n      enrichment happens in the caller - checked by the enrichment living in\n      `_engine_support/reads.py` and the unit tests passing on hand-built states.\n- [ ] existing full-text search and the predicate/filter grammar are unchanged - checked by\n      the pre-existing search tests staying green with zero edits.","id":"dfe3-7ea0-44f4-430b","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"rebar search does not match by ticket id, alias, or bound Jira key"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785867083934717001,"uuid":"7f4e9c41-1b78-47b2-83bc-cf8b8e9f92f3"}